# goal Keep conversations alive without leaking logins

Retaining an ACP session is what makes `previous_response_id` mean anything, and it
is also the only place in this program that holds a resource indefinitely. Both
halves are the point: retain enough to continue a conversation, never enough to
strand a child process nobody can reach.

# invariant Responses chains are linear and snapshots are separate {#conversation_unit}

One ACP session serves a whole Responses chain, but only its latest successfully
stored response id may continue it. `claimResponse` distinguishes a retained older
id as `stale`, an owned tip as `busy`, and a newly acquired tip as `claimed`.
Unknown, evicted and expired ids return null. This prevents an old id from aliasing
the newer agent history and pretending to create a branch.

Stored response bodies have a separate lifecycle. An older retained response stays
readable through GET even though it is stale for continuation. Deleting the current
tip or completing a continued turn with `store:false` closes the ACP conversation
and its bench but preserves older bodies until ordinary bounded snapshot eviction.
Those orphaned snapshots cannot continue because their conversation no longer
exists.

# invariant Every path out of the store closes the ACP session {#no_leaks}

Expiry, eviction, explicit delete, discard-on-failure, and shutdown all funnel
through the same private close. A new exit path that forgets to is not a leak of
memory — it is a logged-in CLI running forever with nothing owning it.

The one that is easy to miss: a turn that **fails before producing a response**
leaves a conversation no id points at. `discard` exists for exactly that, and
`server.js` calls it on the error path.

The store also owns the `sessions_live` gauge. Open and revive add resident
sessions; park and close remove them. These are the same transitions that mutate
`conv.session`, so recording elsewhere would permit the resource and its gauge to
drift apart. The metric removes an agent's series at zero rather than emitting a
zero-only series.

# rule Continuity turns a stateless caller into a conversation {#continuity}

The OpenAI API asks every client to be stateless: resend the whole history, every
time. Taken literally that restarts the agent on each message — it re-reads a
growing transcript and loses the working state it had built up, which for an agent
(a plan, open files, its own subagents) is most of what it knows.

So an incoming history is matched against live sessions by **longest prefix**, and
only the unheard tail is sent. Three properties make this safe rather than clever:

- **Divergence matches nothing.** An edited, branched or trimmed history simply
  fails to match and gets a fresh session. That is the correct answer, not a
  fallback — reusing a session there would drop the agent into a conversation that
  never happened.
- **The preamble is identity, not a turn.** System and developer messages are sent
  once, when the session opens, and a changed preamble prevents a match: continuing
  under a brief the caller has withdrawn would be a lie.
- **A busy session never starts a second prompt.** It may be observed for a 409,
  steered under `busy: queue`, or adopted by the matching result for a suspended
  tool turn; none of those starts an overlapping `session/prompt`. Eviction also
  must not close work already paid for.

# decision Fingerprint semantic messages, not their wire serialization {#canonical_message}

Every digest and message comparison goes through the single `canonicalMessage`
form. It keeps `role`; normalizes a string and one `{type: "text", text}` part to
the same content; preserves multi-part order; and reduces other parts to only the
fields the prompt renderer reads: image URL, or a file's id, data and filename.
Tool calls keep id, type, function name and the **raw arguments string**, plus the
message's `tool_call_id` and `name`. Text is never trimmed or otherwise rewritten.

Raw arguments are deliberately not parsed and re-serialized. Two strings can be
JSON-equivalent while still being different bytes the agent emitted; treating them
as one conversation is a collision with no way to recover, while failing to match
merely opens a fresh session. The same conservative rule makes multi-part order and
every character of text significant.

Provider annotations, refusal nulls, streamed call `index` and unknown message or
part keys are excluded. They are transport or provider metadata the agent never
hears, so a proxy adding them must not silently defeat continuity.

# invariant What is matched is what the agent heard {#prompt_fingerprint_lockstep}

`canonicalMessage`'s content fields mirror `openai.js#toPromptBlocks`, including
its `contentToText` and file reading. If that renderer begins reading a new part
type or field, canonicalization must add it in the same change; if it stops reading
one, identity must stop depending on it. Otherwise prefix matching would claim two
prompts are the same after sending the agent different input, or restart a session
over metadata the agent never received.

A suspended tool turn is the deliberate result-bearing path through busy matching. Its
stored prefix is the pre-suspension conversation followed by the exact assistant
message emitted to the caller: `{role: "assistant", content, tool_calls}`. A
headerless caller resends that prefix and appends trailing `role: "tool"` results;
`matchPrefix` must return the pending turn so those results resume it rather than
opening a second session. Once accepted, the result-bearing request becomes the
pending turn's prefix, and the final assistant message is appended normally.

Streaming `tool_calls` entries carry `index` for an SDK accumulator; non-streaming
messages do not. That field is transport assembly metadata, not conversation
identity, so `fingerprint` discards it. A headerless client may resend either the
SDK's accumulated message or a message assembled directly from deltas and both must
reach the same suspended turn.

The recorded prefix includes the answer we produced, because it comes back as an
`assistant` message next time. If the caller alters that text — redaction,
truncation — the fingerprint stops matching and the shorter prefix wins: one
redundant message, not a wrong conversation. That graceful step down is the reason
for longest-prefix matching rather than exact bookkeeping.

Reuse is logged. Silence would be the failure mode otherwise: a caller whose
preamble carries a timestamp would never match, continuity would quietly never
happen, and nothing would say so.

# invariant Fingerprint generations never mix {#fingerprint_epoch}

Conversation prefixes exist only in this process's in-memory `SessionStore`.
Restarting the server clears the store, and one running JavaScript image cannot
change `canonicalMessage` underneath records it already created. An old-format and
new-format prefix therefore cannot coexist; fingerprint changes need no migration
path or compatibility fallback.

# invariant Suspended turns are reachable and self-releasing {#suspended_lifecycle}

The lifecycle is **find → resume → settle → release**. While a turn is inside a
caller tool, its conversation stays busy but remains findable by a handed-over
`tool_call_id`, the caller's explicit key, or the recorded prefix described above.
Chat lookup uses that order: id first, then key, then prefix. Accepting the trailing
tool results advances that same turn; it never starts a second ACP prompt.

Caller cooperation is not required for release. `setPending` watches the turn
itself. If it settles after its HTTP request detached — tool deadline, ordinary
finish or error — the store clears both `pending` and `busy`, refreshes `lastUsed`,
and normal parking/forgetting rules apply. Every path that clears `pending` also
notifies the ToolBridge so its live call-id index clears before the conversation
can close or be reused. It must not release while a request is attached and shaping
that settled outcome, because a new prompt could otherwise race the response still
being finished.

Every suspended conversation must therefore satisfy both halves: at least one of
key or prefix can reach it, and the turn promise will eventually make it eligible
for pruning even if no caller returns.

# invariant Matching and claiming are one synchronous operation {#atomic_claim}

`matchKey`, `matchPrefix` and `claimResponse` set `busy` before returning a FREE
conversation. There is no awaitable gap between deciding a session is available
and owning its next turn, including when the session is parked and must later be
resumed. The owner releases in `finally`, except while `pending` still represents
the same suspended turn.

A Responses claim additionally returns an opaque `claimId`.
`releaseResponseClaim(convId, claimId)` releases only that owner; a mismatched or
late release cannot clear a newer turn's ownership.

A pending Responses turn is also claimed here, synchronously: only an unattached
pending object with no current owner receives a token. The claim sets
`pending.attached` before returning. A concurrent duplicate observes `busy`, and
releasing the token clears attachment but deliberately leaves `busy` true while
the tool turn remains suspended.

A BUSY result is observation, not another claim. Keyed queue/409 handling and the
pending-result path must see the existing owner without changing it. `peekKey`,
`peekPrefix`, `peekPending` and `find` are explicitly non-claiming; `peekPending`
returns the same pending-observation shape as a busy `matchKey` result so the
existing resume path can adopt the running turn without claiming it again. Use
read-only lookup only when no new ACP turn can follow, such as an inject-only probe,
call-id resume, or response-store inspection. A new lookup API that is neither
claiming nor explicitly read-only is wrong.

# decision A keyed busy conversation refuses instead of forking {#keyed_busy_refusal}

Under `server.busy: fork`, `matchKey` returns the BUSY observation and the server
answers 409 `conversation_busy`. The caller supplied a key to assert that both
requests are the same conversation; starting a second session would contradict
that identity, and two turns cannot share the claimed session. The old behavior
also rebound the key to the new session, orphaning the still-running conversation
so its next continuation could no longer reach it.

Headerless prefix matching is different: it makes no explicit identity claim and
continues to skip busy conversations, so that request may open a session of its
own. `server.busy: queue` is unchanged and may steer into the named running turn
when the agent advertises support.

# openquestion Rename `server.busy: fork`

The value name no longer describes its behavior for a keyed busy conversation;
`refuse` would be clearer. Renaming or adding a value is a configuration-
compatibility decision and is deliberately not part of this change.

# rule Going quiet costs a session, never a conversation {#parking}

A conversation past its TTL is **parked**, not closed: `session/close` gives back
the ACP session — with the login and the memory it was holding — and the record
keeps `sessionId`, so the next message restores it with `session/resume`.

This is the difference between a thread going quiet for an hour and a thread
losing everything it had read. It also re-points the two old bounds at what they
were always really about: `maxSessions` counts RESIDENT sessions, so a store full
of parked records is not under the pressure the cap exists to relieve, and
`sessionTtlMs` decides when to give a session back rather than when to forget.
`forgetTtlMs` is what finally drops the record.

**`resume`, not `load`.** `session/load` replays the whole transcript back as
`session/update` notifications, which this bridge would re-emit as fresh output on
every revive. `resume` restores the session and its MCP connections and replays
nothing.

**Parking is unconditional and self-correcting.** Asking an agent whether it can
resume means starting its process during a prune to find out. Instead every idle
conversation parks; an agent that cannot resume simply fails the revive,
`resumeSession` returns null. Chat may retire it and cold-start because the request
resends the transcript. Responses has no such recovery material: failed resume
retires the chain and returns `404 not_found`; it must never open an empty
replacement session.

The conversation stores the exact caller-owned resume arguments used at open time,
including the caller bench MCP entry. Resume passes those arguments unchanged.
`Agent` adds its configured cwd and MCP baseline when it builds the complete
fingerprint; the store must not try to duplicate that agent-layer normalization.
Chat and Responses both store this context; omitting it for Chat would let a
temporary per-turn reasoning override become the baseline after resume.

**What is never done: `session/delete`.** Dropping our record must not destroy the
agent's own stored transcript. Whose history that is, is the agent's business.

# rule Eviction is by last use, never by age {#lru}

A long conversation someone is actively continuing must outlive an abandoned one
started later. Only a valid latest-id continuation refreshes Responses retention;
a stale lookup cannot keep a chain alive. Every lookup checks the forget deadline
before refresh, so touching an already expired id never resurrects it.

`prune` runs before every new conversation, so the store cannot grow between
requests, and it applies TTL first and the cap second — expiry is about
abandonment, the cap is about pressure, and they are not the same question.

**Neither is about the SIZE of the session, and that is a third question.** A TTL
bounds resident processes; it does not bound a transcript. A conversation continued
every few minutes for a week is exactly the one these rules keep alive, and exactly
the one that walks into the agent's own compaction (a model call out of the same
subscription) and then into a context window no retry recovers.

So `maxContextFill` retires on fullness, read from `usage_update`'s `used`/`size`.
Three properties make it safe rather than clever:

- **Silence is not evidence of room.** An agent that never sends `usage_update`
  leaves `fill` null and keeps its sessions. Guessing would retire healthy sessions
  on every agent that does not implement the update.
- **The last reading wins, not the highest.** A compaction genuinely frees room, and
  a session that just compacted must not stay retired for a peak it came down from.
- **Retirement and closure are separate steps.** `matchKey` and `matchPrefix` stop
  offering a full session immediately; `prune` closes it on the next open. So the
  caller gets a fresh session under the same key on the very next turn, and the old
  one is not left holding a login for a conversation nobody can continue.

Related: the cache is no reason to hold a session resident. Prompt caching lives on
the provider's side with its own TTL, refreshed by use; a live child process warms
nothing. What continuity actually buys is that the agent does not re-orient from
scratch.

# rule Conversation and response storage are independently finite {#storage_bounds}

`maxSessions` bounds resident ACP sessions. It is not a storage limit.
`maxConversations` defaults to 1,000 retained conversation records;
`maxResponses` defaults to 1,000 serialized response bodies; and
`maxResponseBytes` defaults to 64 MiB. `prepareOpen` performs deterministic cleanup
and evicts the least-recently-used idle conversation when needed. If every slot is
active it throws `SessionCapacityError` with code `session_capacity`; active or
pending turns are never cancelled merely to admit another conversation. The error
has HTTP status 503 for the server's normal error mapper.

`prepareOpen` returns an admission token and reserves its slot across the
asynchronous ACP `openSession`. `open(..., {admissionId})` consumes that token;
`cancelOpen(admissionId)` releases it when ACP opening fails. Unreserved opens count
outstanding admissions and cannot steal their capacity. Admission decisions are
serialized through eviction so concurrent callers cannot both reserve one freed slot.

`record` serializes once, measures `Buffer.byteLength` of those exact bytes, then
stores a recursively frozen parse of that serialization. It never retains the
caller's mutable reference, so later SSE assembly or caller mutation cannot change
GET data or exceed admitted bytes.
It may evict oldest stale snapshots, including the prior tip of the same chain,
but never another conversation's current tip. If the new body itself is too large
or only current tips remain, it throws `ResponseStorageError` with code
`response_storage_full` without changing the previous tip or byte/count totals.
The HTTP layer must handle this after agent work explicitly; it must not report a
stored response that GET cannot retrieve. The error has HTTP status 507 to separate
local retention exhaustion from agent or subscription failure.

# invariant Cleanup has one owner and no process-lifetime handle {#periodic_cleanup}

`cleanup(agents)` deduplicates overlapping passes and is awaitable for tests.
`startCleanup` uses an unref'ed interval, and `stopCleanup` clears it and awaits an
in-progress pass. `closeAll` stops cleanup before closing every conversation and
permanently closes the store. All paths that drop a conversation still funnel
through the cleanup hooks that release pending calls and caller benches.

# invariant Retirement has a closed cause and a bounded attribution {#retirement_attribution}

`retire` accepts exactly `context_fill`, `forgotten`, `revive_failed`,
`dead_session`, `abandoned_tool_turn`, or `late_results`; an unknown token throws.
The vocabulary is shared by the metric and `x_acp2api.session.reason`. In
particular, the forget deadline is `forgotten`, never `ttl`: an ordinary TTL expiry
parks a resumable conversation and is not retirement.

A replayable Chat conversation records `{key?, prefixFingerprints?, agentName,
reason, at}` as it retires. Key matching is exact. Prefix matching compares the
existing canonical fingerprints as `[systemId, ...prefix]`, chooses the longest
matching prefix (newest on a tie), and never invents a second message comparison
form. A matching fresh session consumes the tombstone and annotates only its first
response; an unrelated fresh conversation consumes nothing.

Tombstones expire after `forgetTtlMs` and the store retains at most 1,000, evicting
the oldest first. Both bounds are necessary: attribution must neither outlive the
conversation-retention policy nor grow with process-lifetime traffic. Responses
conversations do not create tombstones because `previous_response_id` is already a
proxy-proof address and exposes retirement as 404.

Generic `discard`, deleting the current Responses tip, `store: false`, and
shutdown are cleanup rather than attributed retirement. A newly opened turn that
fails before any successful response likewise has nothing a later request can
truthfully call a replay.

# rule Pruning uses an injected registry and clock {#prune}

`prune(agents)` receives the registry so a closing conversation can reach its
agent; this module does not depend on the agent layer. `now` is injected. Tests
drive expiry and LRU by moving a number, never by sleeping.

# test sessions

- an expired conversation is closed by `prune` and stops resolving; lookup at the
  deadline cannot refresh it
- with the cap exceeded, the least recently *used* conversation is evicted — a
  refreshed old one survives while a newer idle one goes
- only the latest stored response can claim a continuation; an older retained id is
  stale but its body remains readable
- claim tokens prevent a wrong owner from releasing a continued turn
- deleting the tip and `store:false` close the chain while preserving older GET snapshots
- response count/byte limits evict stale snapshots deterministically and reject an
  oversized body atomically
- conversation admission evicts idle LRU records and rejects capacity filled by active work
- admission remains reserved across asynchronous session opening and is cancellable on failure
- resume metadata retains instructions and caller MCP arguments through parking
- periodic cleanup is non-overlapping, unref'ed and stopped by shutdown
- a growing history continues one session; only the unheard tail is sent
- a diverging history, and a changed system preamble, each start a fresh session
- proxy-only content-shape and annotation changes continue headerlessly, while an
  edited message still starts fresh
- a busy conversation is neither matched nor evicted
- a pending conversation is matched by its emitted tool-call prefix
- an unattended pending turn clears busy when it settles and can then be pruned
- retirement tombstones match keys and canonical prefixes once, expire with `forgetTtlMs`, and evict oldest at 1,000
- Responses conversations never create Chat replay tombstones
